# =============================================================================
# state.manifest — what `scripts/fks-state.sh export` bundles into an encrypted
# snapshot. Copy to `state.manifest` (gitignored) and tune to your deployment.
#
# Lines are either:
#   • a file or glob, relative to the repo root — optionally prefixed `file:`
#     (e.g. .env, models/*.bin). Sibling-repo paths (`../fks-state/...`) work:
#     the snapshot is anchored at the PARENT directory of this checkout, so
#     `..` paths round-trip through export/import.
#   • db:<database>[:table1,table2,...]           dumped via the postgres container
#   • vol:<docker-volume-name>                     archived from a named volume
#     Use this for on-disk state that lives ONLY in a volume — a bot journal, a
#     checkpoint dir. `status` warns about any fks* volume no vol: line covers,
#     so run it after adding a service.
# Blank lines and #-comments are ignored. Missing file matches are skipped with
# a warning, so it's safe to list paths you don't always have.
#
# NOTHING here should ever be committed to the public repo — this manifest only
# names the private surface; the snapshot itself lives in the private fks-state
# repo, age-encrypted.
# =============================================================================

# ── Secrets & TLS ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.env
.env.secrets
infrastructure/certs/

# ── Strategy configs — the edge (the sibling bot repos' tuned params) ────────
# These live in the sibling checkouts (the `crypto` repo is dissolved: the spot
# bot lives in fks-spawner, the futures/funding edges in the private
# fks-state). Point at them directly — `../` paths are supported. Uncomment
# what applies:
# ../fks-state/bots/crypto-futures/funding.toml
# ../fks-spawner/bots/spot-portfolio/spot-portfolio.toml

# ── Model weights / optimizer + backtest output ──────────────────────────────
models/*.bin
models/*.safetensors
models/*.pt
models/*.onnx

# ── Live file-state (journals, open positions) ───────────────────────────────
# Bot working state that must survive a redeploy. Adjust to your layout:
# ../fks-state/bots/crypto-futures/funding-paper.jsonl
# ../fks-state/bots/crypto-futures/funding-state-*.json
# ../fks-spawner/bots/spot-portfolio/spot-trades.jsonl

# ── Database tables — the runtime secret store + account/PnL/risk/auth state ─
# Dumped data-only (schema lives in src/sql/, applied at container init). A
# --data-only restore requires the table's CREATE to exist on the target, so
# ONLY list tables backed by a src/sql/spawner migration (or janus bootstrap).
db:fks_db:exchange_secrets
db:janus_db:api_keys,api_key_audit
# Funding bot state (schema: 014_funding_state.sql). framework_risk_state +
# funding_kill_switch are the LIVE risk state — the SessionPnl/CircuitBreaker
# halt and the kill sentinel; excluding them means a DR restore silently
# re-arms a halted strategy with a cleared loss window (review H3). The two
# ledger tables carry the paper PnL + open positions.
db:fks_db:framework_risk_state,funding_kill_switch
db:fks_db:funding_open_trades,funding_paper_records
# WebUI auth + audit (schema: 010/011/012 + 013). webui_users restores the
# admin account (without it a restore boots to bootstrap mode — a CSPRNG admin
# is reseeded, but the operators/invites/history are gone); webui_auth_audit +
# notification_log are append-only forensic ledgers. webui_sessions +
# webui_alert_acks included so a restore preserves live sessions + the
# ack inbox rather than logging everyone out / re-surfacing acked alerts.
db:fks_db:webui_users,webui_sessions,webui_auth_audit
db:fks_db:webui_invites,webui_alert_acks
db:fks_db:notification_log
# Add more account / position / pnl tables as your schema grows, e.g.:
# db:fks_db:bot_configs,bot_runs,notification_channels
# db:fks_db:accounts,transfers,net_worth_snapshots     (treasury layer)
# db:fks_db:edges,backtest_runs                        (edge factory)
# db:fks_db:ui_layouts                                 (saved workspace layouts)
# db:fks_db:rithmic_accounts                           (016 — which Rithmic
#   logins exist, what each is for, and which single account is hand-traded.
#   No secrets: those are in exchange_secrets under `rithmic:<id>`.)
# db:janus_db:signals                                  (the signal history the
#   Qdrant vectors can be rebuilt from — which is WHY that 480 MB volume is
#   deliberately not archived below.)

# ── Docker volumes — state that exists ONLY on disk ──────────────────────────
# `status` lists every fks* volume no line here covers. Most SHOULD stay
# uncovered; the point is that the choice is deliberate rather than forgotten.
#
# vol:fks-bot-state-crypto-spot
#   The live-money spot bot's `spot-trades.jsonl`. 8 KB, and the ONLY record of
#   those fills: unlike the funding bot (FR_STATE_BACKEND=postgres ->
#   funding_paper_records) the spot bot has no database backend, so /profit is
#   computed from a file that exists nowhere else.
# vol:fks_janus_checkpoints
#   Trained model checkpoints; expensive to regenerate.
# vol:fks_redis_data
#   Shared state + the kill sentinel's cache layer.
#
# DELIBERATELY NOT ARCHIVED, and why — the snapshot travels by git, and that
# push has already failed once at ~180 MB:
#   fks_qdrant_data   (~480 MB) rebuildable from db:janus_db:signals
#   fks_questdb_data  (~430 MB) dominated by candles_crypto, all re-fetchable
#                     from the exchanges. NOTE candles_futures is the exception:
#                     a few thousand rows, and Rithmic does NOT backfill, so
#                     that history is gone if the volume is. It wants its own
#                     small export rather than archiving the whole volume.
#   prometheus/grafana/loki/alertmanager  observability, re-derivable
#   fks_postgres_data lives in the db: dumps above, not as a raw volume
